#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078149 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/id #uuid "a3234c09-08eb-56f2-8a4c-d6430d38c356" :question/math-academy-id "e-275" :question/problem "A spherical balloon is being inflated so that its volume is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing with respect to the radius at the moment when the diameter of the balloon is equal to $12\\, \\text{cm}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037675/assets/e03fb046f74771676d7b1e83a1cf09176adcffaf8112e9de263404b8be74b026.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the diameter of the balloon is $12\\, \\text{cm},$ its radius is $r =6\\, \\text{cm}.$ Now, we know that the volume of a sphere with radius $r$  is given by \n\n$$\nV= V(r) = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $V(r)$  with respect to $r$ is \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\nV'(r) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d r}\\left(  \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\\right)\\[5pt]\n &  =  \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi (3r^2)\\[5pt]\n &= 4\\pi r^2, \n}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $r=6$ gives\n\n$$\nV'(6)=  4\\pi (6)^2= 144\\pi.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing when its diameter is $12\\, \\text{cm}\\,$ is $\\, 144\\pi \\, \\text{cm}^3/\\text{cm}.$\n\n**Note:** We read $\\text{cm}^3/\\text{cm}$ as \"centimeters cubed per centimeter\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078150 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "3c4f9912-4b08-5e11-8ef6-4e4ee35765c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-276" :question/problem "Lizzie buys $b$ pounds of bananas at a price $p,$ in dollars, that can be modeled by the function $p(b)=5e^{-0.2b}.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change of the price when $b=5$ pounds?" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the price when $b=5$ pounds is equal to $p'(5).$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(b) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}b}(5{e}^{-0.2b}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{-0.2b}\\cdot (-0.2) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-0.2b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $b=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(5) & =-{e}^{-0.2(5)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the price when $b=5$ pounds is $-\\dfrac{1}{e}$ dollars per pound."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078251 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "236cec41-4836-5526-893d-e6f2a23d1801" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f2eda994-e00a-5e9b-a26d-720d01e6fa07" :question/math-academy-id "e-6174" :question/problem "A particle moves in a straight line. After $t$ seconds the particle is $\\, f(t)=2t^3 - 5t + 9\\,$ feet away from its initial position. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position as a function of $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position is equal to $f'(t).$ \n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-5t+9) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $t$ seconds, the instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position is $6t^2 - 5$ feet per second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "838cf9ad-6de2-55f4-a99c-8657f56e70ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{4\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297615} {:answer/id #uuid "8c248248-84d5-57d4-b6ce-fe4a85f98373" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297616} {:answer/id #uuid "7fcbd2af-f496-5ae2-aff8-1c1a8c03dcfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297617} {:answer/id #uuid "2e8e9cc9-632f-59bf-914f-6bcbd2efd7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2C}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297618} {:answer/id #uuid "e78e8e43-abd8-5cf1-8c86-fd1003f57849" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fcbd2af-f496-5ae2-aff8-1c1a8c03dcfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297617} :answer-field/id #uuid "316d79eb-c7b6-5349-bbfa-8d92c0c4eeba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93b93b20-63af-5abb-b512-c02cecaedf81" :question/math-academy-id "q-2545" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037675/assets/f59f65e184ccf4fdf2ae2124543ece45d41afce09d743dec9f17730dd00124d4.png)\n\nThe area $A$ of the circle above is increasing. The circumference and radius of the circle are $C$ and $r$ respectively. \nFind the rate of change of the area of the circle with respect to its circumference." :question/worked-solution "The area of a circle is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the circle.\n\nWe know that $C=2\\pi r.$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the circumference $C$ by substituting $r=\\frac{C}{2\\pi }$ in the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $A(C)$ with respect to $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(C) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}({C}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }(2C) \\\\  & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7874d3c1-1e7d-5935-918f-98a39cbf1ad9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2s^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286681} {:answer/id #uuid "15d53ce7-5bf9-5cbc-801d-aca9e585a89b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6s^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7874d3c1-1e7d-5935-918f-98a39cbf1ad9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2s^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286681} :answer-field/id #uuid "a76f6768-6175-5831-9bef-cf3c3433a53d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef0e7ebd-8eb3-5e6b-8197-77c0558d373a" :question/math-academy-id "q-313364" :question/problem "The volume of a right square pyramid is increasing. Given that its height $h$ is always twice the length of a base, find the rate of change of the volume of the pyramid with respect to its base length $s.$\n\n*Hint*: The volume of a right square pyramid is $V=\\frac{1}{3}{s}^{2}h.$ \n\nThe rate of change of the pyramid’s volume with respect to $s$ is \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We are given that $h=2s,$ so we can express the volume of the pyramid $V$ as a function of the base edge length $s$ by substituting $h=2s$ in the formula of the volume. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(s) & =\\frac{1}{3}{s}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{s}^{2}(2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{s}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $V(s)$ with respect to $s$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{d}{ds}\\,(\\frac{2}{3}{s}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{d}{ds}\\,({s}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3{s}^{2} \\\\  & =2{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the rate of change of the pyramid’s volume with respect to its base length $s$ is $2{s}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "236cec41-4836-5526-893d-e6f2a23d1801" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9537d5e3-26d5-5698-96a6-c53109fc8e5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(t+1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297622} {:answer/id #uuid "b6934440-20a2-590b-a245-66e7f0a746d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9537d5e3-26d5-5698-96a6-c53109fc8e5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(t+1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297622} :answer-field/id #uuid "12f72ab5-e1eb-5d08-8081-32ef946fbe1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2e07b371-489a-5311-9833-37b80a53596c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343166" :question/problem "The funding $F,$ in millions of dollars, allocated to a research project $t$ years after it begins is modeled by\n\n$$\nF(t)=5+(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWhat is the instantaneous rate of change of the funding as a function of $t?$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change of the funding is\n{{field-1}} million dollars per year." :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the funding is ${F}^{′}(t).$\n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}[5+(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+(t+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{t+1} \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the funding is $1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)$ million dollars per year."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "236cec41-4836-5526-893d-e6f2a23d1801" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b79824f-0d58-561b-a13e-7f5242c46a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2te^{-t}-t^2e^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b79824f-0d58-561b-a13e-7f5242c46a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2te^{-t}-t^2e^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297626} :answer-field/id #uuid "956351f3-e5a9-506b-b875-9603998a6bb0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3771af58-f6ea-5a0e-8fb6-883a58c8196f" :question/math-academy-id "q-343167" :question/problem "The inventory level $I,$ in hundreds of units, of a medical supply $t$ weeks after ordering begins, is modeled by\n\n$$\nI(t)={t}^{2}{e}^{-t}+12,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWhat is the instantaneous rate of change of the inventory level as a function of $t?$ \n\nThe instantaneous rate of change of the inventory level is\n{{field-1}} hundreds of units per week." :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the inventory level is ${I}^{′}(t).$\n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}[{t}^{2}{e}^{-t}+12] \\\\  & =2t{e}^{-t}+{t}^{2}\\cdot (-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =2t{e}^{-t}-{t}^{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}(2-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the inventory level is $t{e}^{-t}(2-t)$ hundreds of units per week."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55742b20-d2eb-52dd-93ba-2bea32a81289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55742b20-d2eb-52dd-93ba-2bea32a81289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266161} :answer-field/id #uuid "181d8716-3c0a-5aff-9c35-c6ef0ef17dc0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bcb5129b-3804-548c-a47a-d6b56b122986" :question/math-academy-id "q-343424" :question/problem "The concentration $C$ (in millimoles per liter) of a chemical in a reactor $t$ minutes after the reaction begins is modeled by the function $C(t)={t}^{3}+25$ for $0\\le t\\le 10.$ Find the instantaneous rate at which the concentration is changing with respect to time at the moment when the concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change when the concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter is\n{{field-1}} millimoles per liter per minute." :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the chemical's concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter, the time can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(t) & =150 \\\\ {t}^{3}+25 & =150 \\\\ {t}^{3} & =125 \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous rate at which the chemical's concentration is changing when $t=5$ minutes is equal to ${C}^{′}(5).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $C(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+25) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=5$ gives\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(5)=3(5)^{2}=75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the chemical's concentration when its concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter is  $75$ millimoles per liter per minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9d523ba-9b37-5749-9a36-a7981cff36a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297629} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc9dae0-08fc-5a43-9634-6a5ab6bd891b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9d523ba-9b37-5749-9a36-a7981cff36a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297629} :answer-field/id #uuid "13cf4b10-a598-5e0e-921f-502564520d45" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "507d7e12-bec8-5924-a111-bf667b784658" :question/math-academy-id "q-343498" :question/problem "The surface area $A$ of a sphere is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the surface area of the sphere is changing with respect to its radius at the moment when the radius of the sphere is equal to $5\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at the moment when its radius is $5$ inches is equal to\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{in}.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that the surface area of a sphere with radius $r$ is given by \n\n$$\nA=A(r)=4\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $A(r)$ with respect to $r$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\pi (2r) \\\\  & =8\\pi r,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $r=5$ gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(5)=8\\pi (5)=40\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the surface area of the sphere is changing when its radius is $5\\,\\text{in}$, is $40\\pi $ ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{in}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbe2982f-a752-54b7-b0f4-c50b37c260b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266168} {:answer/id #uuid "c48d34f1-d84d-51c7-96b0-c287ce05a6d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbe2982f-a752-54b7-b0f4-c50b37c260b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266168} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e113cfa-03c5-531e-9d48-c21ef10c1193" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce974f27-56cb-5d6d-a571-c1f4112d1075" :question/math-academy-id "q-343502" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037675/assets/5fc1aff711355a147abac33a513434c55575c552ab58f0164ebc87222530f371.png)\n\nThe volume of a cube-shaped container is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the container is changing with respect to the side length at the moment when the face diagonal of the cube is equal to $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at the moment when the face diagonal is $10\\sqrt{2}$ centimeters is equal to\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the face diagonal of the cube is $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}$, its side length $s$ is $10\\,\\text{cm}.$ Now, the volume of a cube with side length $s$ is given by \n\n$$\nV=V(s)={s}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $V(s)$ with respect to $s$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}({s}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{s}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $s=10$ gives\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(10)=3(10)^{2}=300.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the container is increasing when the face diagonal is $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}$ is $300\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9302bc57-0b92-57ac-ac8a-40346871ae5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,695$ computers per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297633} {:answer/id #uuid "b02029e3-c2e5-5cae-8e1e-987d224a052d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,699$ computers per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297634} {:answer/id #uuid "dc73284c-f8d5-5ff3-b9bd-96d45f3598be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,495$ computers per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297635} {:answer/id #uuid "53d48ccb-79d3-5295-9847-af807c0197c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,501$ computers per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297636} {:answer/id #uuid "e0cc9bae-b4a6-56f2-8dd2-682f8c8008c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,801$ computers per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b02029e3-c2e5-5cae-8e1e-987d224a052d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,699$ computers per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297634} :answer-field/id #uuid "b314822c-8502-5d9c-96db-55ae51a7bb14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4df84652-8096-5b0a-ac62-74634ce27764" :question/math-academy-id "q-48700" :question/problem "The number of computers affected by the spread of some ransomware after $t$ days can be modeled by the function $f(t)=\\frac{100\\,({t}^{3}+2)}{t+1}.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change in the number of infected computers when $t=9$ days?" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change in the spread when $t=9$ days is equal to ${f}^{′}(9).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{100\\,({t}^{3}+2)}{t+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{100(3{t}^{2})(t+1)-100({t}^{3}+2)(1)}{{(t+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-2}{{(t+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(9) & =100\\cdot \\frac{2(9)^{3}+3(9)^{2}-2}{{((9)+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{1\\,458+243-2}{100} \\\\  & =1\\,699.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change in the spread when $t=9$ is $1\\,699$ computers per day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3b19f6b-d5db-5615-bdec-a86c99773854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297640} {:answer/id #uuid "0c2cc91f-1d24-59d8-8e2f-34d800d495e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,600\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297641} {:answer/id #uuid "f2443660-7bd3-5940-b5e3-362e8ac4ce51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297642} {:answer/id #uuid "6404aa9b-f9e2-5e42-b886-2a1142be79e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297643} {:answer/id #uuid "ed587da6-042b-5ee8-852e-c07fb7353538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3b19f6b-d5db-5615-bdec-a86c99773854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297640} :answer-field/id #uuid "b395a869-7c8e-5438-8347-1bdf9c83021e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0340e2b4-a8dc-5b2e-8637-96bee21fbe98" :question/math-academy-id "q-48728" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037675/assets/d56c10dfa022454135ddafd4255f737182bdca186b99ed1e686132447341bff8.png)\n\nA spherical balloon is being inflated so that its volume is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing with respect to the radius at the moment when the diameter of the balloon is equal to $20\\,\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the diameter of the spherical balloon is $20\\,\\text{cm}$,  its radius $r$ is $10\\,\\text{cm}.$ Now, the volume of a sphere with radius $r$  is given by \n\n$$\nV=V(r)=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $V(r)$ with respect to $r$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi (3{r}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\pi {r}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $r=10$ gives\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(10)=4\\pi (10)^{2}=400\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is increasing when the diameter is $20\\,\\text{cm}$ is $400\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff3ea3a0-0252-57e3-aeec-232b99135da4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262338} {:answer/id #uuid "96f05212-4e3a-599e-92ef-43d04918b0f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ meter per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262339} {:answer/id #uuid "1e3f02ef-59c7-5837-a6c2-9d92c02c90d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262340} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fb16e8-cf8a-5273-9f9e-97b4fbe73eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262341} {:answer/id #uuid "0445507a-2423-5178-bf12-706b5c412c22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff3ea3a0-0252-57e3-aeec-232b99135da4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262338} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e1d0e37-098a-5943-85a3-515d49a429e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e527e4e-1ea7-5d39-a401-6a8d7982a17f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72352" :question/problem "The function $x(t)={t}^{2}+t$ models the distance traveled by a car $t$ seconds after it sets off on a journey. Find the instantaneous rate at which the distance traveled by the car is changing with respect to time when $t=1$ second." :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate at which the distance traveled by the car is changing when $t=1$ second is equal to ${x}^{′}(1).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $x(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)=2t+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=1$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(1)=2(1)+1=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the distance traveled by the car is changing when $t=1$ second is $3$ meters per second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "469086ff-5fd5-54a7-bfc0-faecc7f5c7e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-50$ degrees Fahrenheit per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297647} {:answer/id #uuid "566cf014-59dd-57f5-b3a0-7b04511b2e46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-72$ degrees Fahrenheit per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297648} {:answer/id #uuid "0381cefa-c2aa-56e1-8f37-1d69d183b280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-108$ degrees Fahrenheit per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297649} {:answer/id #uuid "02baa62e-d511-5d50-846b-133b571bc3b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-54$ degrees Fahrenheit per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297650} {:answer/id #uuid "3deab803-29e9-5aac-b603-3c7f8f038bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-36$ degrees Fahrenheit per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0381cefa-c2aa-56e1-8f37-1d69d183b280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-108$ degrees Fahrenheit per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297649} :answer-field/id #uuid "bad2a0a6-bc28-5701-b465-2fb9a735e0eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af3a3dc0-c338-5ee0-b1a3-cea63f01a521" :question/math-academy-id "q-72356" :question/problem "The temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit, of a rapidly cooling substance after $t$ minutes can be modeled by the function $F(t)=250-{t}^{3}.$ Find the instantaneous rate at which the temperature of the substance is changing with respect to time at the moment when the temperature of the substance is equal to $34$ degrees Fahrenheit." :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the temperature of the substance is $34$ degrees Fahrenheit, the time can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}34 & =250-{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{3} & =216 \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous rate at which the temperature of the substance is changing when $t=6$ minutes is equal to ${F}^{′}(6).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $F(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250-{t}^{3})=-3{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=6$ gives\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(6)=-3(6)^{2}=-108.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the temperature of the substance is changing when $t=6$ minutes is $-108$ degrees Fahrenheit per minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "236cec41-4836-5526-893d-e6f2a23d1801" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43329b9d-e113-5c0f-a41e-cfeead79d7b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{169}{5}$ degrees of Fahrenheit per degree of Celsius" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297654} {:answer/id #uuid "46ff578c-cac4-563d-8412-e9062ca9639f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{9}{5}$ degrees of Fahrenheit per degree of Celsius" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297655} {:answer/id #uuid "1c71d900-2229-5eea-a385-492c5d4668dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{169}$ degrees of Fahrenheit per degree of Celsius" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297656} {:answer/id #uuid "a40460e7-1186-569b-b39c-95251f5fe61a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{293}{9}$ degrees of Fahrenheit per degree of Celsius" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297657} {:answer/id #uuid "2a86e6bf-b123-54c8-ad39-b49fdd73e2ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{9}$ degrees of Fahrenheit per degree of Celsius" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46ff578c-cac4-563d-8412-e9062ca9639f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{9}{5}$ degrees of Fahrenheit per degree of Celsius" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297655} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b62f26e-2cfb-5faf-8c53-54f20df0dca0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60bc5656-b32e-5701-901a-41c0c8fd54d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-72357" :question/problem "The air temperature $F$ in degrees Fahrenheit can be modeled by the function $F(C)=\\frac{9}{5}C+32,$ where $C$ is the air temperature in degrees Celsius. Find the instantaneous rate of change of the air temperature in degrees Fahrenheit with respect to the air temperature in degrees Celsius." :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the air temperature in degrees Fahrenheit with respect to the air temperature in degrees Celsius is equal to ${F}^{′}(C).$\n\nNow, the rate of change of $F(C)$ with respect to $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(C) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{9}{5}C+32) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the air temperature in degrees Fahrenheit with respect to the air temperature in degrees Celsius is $\\frac{9}{5}$ degrees Fahrenheit per degree of Celsius."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26ce2d59-0559-5532-b34b-752dc89fae5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-9{e}^{-3}$ liters per dollar" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297661} {:answer/id #uuid "6a286758-9831-5d20-a330-8972dda43713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-10e$ liters per dollar" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297662} {:answer/id #uuid "65c7cc0f-e99c-5b77-9062-0b10f09a6dd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-12{e}^{-9}$ liters per dollar" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297663} {:answer/id #uuid "ea27b3f0-cf48-516c-86e4-2d58d94865fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-8{e}^{-9}$ liters per dollar" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297664} {:answer/id #uuid "bc09f5e2-b539-5e88-960a-7ec85bfdb99e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6{e}^{9}$ liters per dollar" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65c7cc0f-e99c-5b77-9062-0b10f09a6dd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-12{e}^{-9}$ liters per dollar" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297663} :answer-field/id #uuid "99c25c86-8a1b-5f85-9930-bc19be1e7305" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eedc7cd2-9ab3-5727-ada2-18fc9f2156b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-72367" :question/problem "The function $g(p)={e}^{-4{p}^{2}}$ models the volume of gas that a vendor can sell, in liters, at a price of $p$ dollars per liter. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of gas is changing with respect to the price when $p=1.50$ dollars." :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate at which the volume of gas that can be sold is changing when $p=1.50$ dollars is equal to ${g}^{′}(1.5).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $g(p)$ with respect to $p$ is \n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(p)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}p}({e}^{-4{p}^{2}})=-8p{e}^{-4{p}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $p=1.5$ gives\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(1.5)=-8(1.5){e}^{-4(1.5)^{2}}=-12{e}^{-9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the volume of gas that can be sold is changing when $p=1.5$ dollars is $-12{e}^{-9}$ liters per dollar."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "236cec41-4836-5526-893d-e6f2a23d1801" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a52c4689-da7a-570e-8989-65b6edf76e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262352} {:answer/id #uuid "27d04d21-825b-59ef-bea0-e7136ff9cae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{355}{1+{e}^{-t}}$ liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262353} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b91f08-9fb4-5b3f-9b4a-1814f2033caf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$355{e}^{t}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262354} {:answer/id #uuid "db14086d-d818-5ca4-a619-9770d3a947e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{710{e}^{t}}{2+{e}^{-t}}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262355} {:answer/id #uuid "43741ee7-1f7c-5b81-9f09-2b2714183729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$710{e}^{-t}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a52c4689-da7a-570e-8989-65b6edf76e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262352} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fde2b24-adb2-54a1-9c8d-6b66f11b4bbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1a7b751-463a-5c53-9c04-73eeed9f0b75" :question/math-academy-id "q-86191" :question/problem "The function $P(t)=710\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{e}^{t})$ represents the volume of milk, in liters, produced on a farm $t$ days after the owner bought new cows. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the volume of produced milk as a function of $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the volume of produced milk with respect to time is ${P}^{′}(t).$\n\nNow, the rate of change of $P(t)$ with respect to $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(710\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =710\\,\\frac{{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the volume of produced milk is  $\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}$ liters per day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "236cec41-4836-5526-893d-e6f2a23d1801" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46de3614-09ff-5e05-825f-cb95073f4d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4{e}^{2t}$ cubic centimeters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297668} {:answer/id #uuid "6125ba32-7495-55be-aa76-d2676bddfffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$16{e}^{t/2}$ cubic centimeters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297669} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a0656b-66e6-5f6e-baf6-d2208a9e7c63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8{e}^{t/2}$ cubic centimeters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297670} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ede6a0-1e3d-5892-aa8d-c45b485c5304" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4{e}^{t/2}$ cubic centimeters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297671} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e89c2c-795a-56a3-84e7-6374b983b768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8{e}^{2t}$ cubic centimeters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8ede6a0-1e3d-5892-aa8d-c45b485c5304" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4{e}^{t/2}$ cubic centimeters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297671} :answer-field/id #uuid "38dbc5fe-ec62-5f64-98b8-52419e45ce5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1cd418e-19aa-59f8-93b4-cb745f14ecef" :question/math-academy-id "q-86483" :question/problem "The function $v(t)=8{e}^{t/2}$ gives the volume, in cubic centimeters, of methylated spirit produced by a chemical process $t$ hours after the process begins. Find the instantaneous rate of change of spirit volume as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the spirit volume is equal to ${v}^{′}(t).$\n\nNow, the rate of change of $v(t)$ with respect to $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{e}^{t/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (8{e}^{t/2}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the volume of spirit with respect to time is $4{e}^{t/2}$ cubic centimeters per hour."}]}
